(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-cp-exam-1-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Clin Path I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which five features define a benign tumour?","choices":["Autonomous growth, clonal origin, parenchyma and stroma, a single genetic alteration, no stimulus needed","Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, mitoses, abnormal differentiation, and invasion","Circumscribed, compresses, often encapsulated, well differentiated, no metastasis, slow","Ragged border, infiltrates surrounding tissue, no capsule, variably differentiated, may metastasize, grows rapidly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — each has a malignant counterpart on the same slide: ragged border, infiltrates and invades, various degrees of differentiation, may metastasize, grows rapidly. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which tinea subtype affects the nails, and what is its other name?","choices":["Tinea unguium, also called onychomycosis","Tinea capitis, also called onychomycosis","Tinea manuum, affecting the nails of the hand","Tinea barbae, affecting nails and beard"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Corporis is trunk and limbs, pedis foot, cruris groin, capitis head, facialis face, manuum hand, barbae beard. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"How do gentamicin and tobramycin damage hearing, and which frequencies go first?","choices":["They cross-link DNA in the stria vascularis, disturbing endolymph ion homeostasis","Reactive oxygen species destroy outer hair cells from the base, so high pitches go first","They alter the stria vascularis potential reversibly","They inhibit the prestin motor protein in outer hair cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The base of the cochlea encodes high frequencies, so basal damage shows as high-frequency loss. The mechanism is oxidative injury, not a direct toxic binding. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the hallmark of dry, atrophic macular degeneration?","choices":["Cotton-wool spots in the nerve fibre layer","Drusen — discrete yellow extracellular debris beneath the pigment epithelium and Bruch membrane","Choroidal neovascularisation breaching upward into the subretinal space","Neovascularisation on the optic disc"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — with its composition and location. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which carcinogens are produced by the combustion of tobacco, and what do they cause?","choices":["Aromatic amines, causing bladder and lung cancer; among the most powerful carcinogens known","Nitrosamines, causing gastric and oesophageal cancer","Aflatoxins, causing hepatocellular carcinoma","Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, causing bladder and lung cancer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — among the most powerful carcinogens known, and tobacco smoke contains numerous others besides. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What does the abbreviation PMN refer to?","choices":["All granulocytes","Neutrophils only","All mononuclear leucocytes","Plasma cells and memory B cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphonuclear leukocyte, abbreviated PMN, is used to refer to all granulocytes rather than to neutrophils alone. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Fibrinous inflammation forms a thick meshwork that can be removed only by what, and what happens if that removal fails?","choices":["Complement fragment C3b; failure results in ulceration","Macrophage digestion; failure results in gangrene","Phagocytosis by neutrophils; failure produces pus","Fibrolytic enzymes; failure results in scar tissue formation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The meshwork can only be removed by fibrolytic enzymes, and failure of this results in scar tissue formation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What happens in familial adenomatosis polyposis syndrome, and by what age does cancer develop?","choices":["Hundreds to thousands of premalignant polyps young, then colon cancer by fifty","Patients develop a single premalignant polyp in childhood, and colon cancer by age thirty","Patients develop multiple neurofibromas at puberty, and sarcoma by age forty","Patients develop skin cancer in sun-exposed areas from early childhood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — five hundred to two thousand five hundred adenomatous polyps in the teens and twenties. The responsible gene is adenomatous polyposis coli. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is a biomaterial in this context?","choices":["Anything not human that is put into a human","Any material that has been sterilised before implantation","Any tissue harvested from the patient's own body","Any protein produced during the inflammatory response"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is the working definition given, and it is why the opsonisation point generalises across implanted devices. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What can a myopic patient see well?","choices":["Objects up close","Objects far away","Neither near nor far without correction","Only objects at the extreme periphery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — myopia is nearsightedness, so near vision is the preserved one. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A temporal lobe lesion affecting part of the optic radiation produces which defect?","choices":["Contralateral inferior quadrantanopsia instead","Bitemporal hemianopsia","Monocular blindness","Contralateral superior quadrantanopsia, or pie in the sky"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — site D, in the temporal loop of the optic radiation. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the two phases of allergic contact dermatitis?","choices":["Irritation, then hyperkeratosis of the stratum corneum","Radial growth, then vertical growth","Sensitization, in which Langerhans cells present haptens to T cells to create specialised memory; and elicitation, in which re-exposure to the same hapten promotes T cell migration and cutaneous inflammation","Sensitization by mast cell degranulation, followed by elicitation through the release of histamine into the surrounding dermal tissue on every subsequent exposure to that same allergen thereafter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a delayed hypersensitivity reaction occurring 48 to 72 hours after exposure. Nickel and poison ivy are the examples. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the sequence by which Eustachian tube dysfunction creates negative middle ear pressure?","choices":["The tympanic membrane becomes stiff and pulls the ossicles inward","The tube fails to open, so the mucosa absorbs the gases","Mucus production increases faster than the tube can drain it","Bacterial infection erodes the tube lining and opens it permanently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The middle ear is an air-filled space; if it is not re-ventilated, the mucosa absorbs the gases and the pressure falls. That negative pressure is the start of the otitis media sequence. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which set of causes belongs to sensorineural rather than conductive loss?","choices":["Eustachian tube dysfunction, middle ear effusion and stapes fixation","Presbycusis, ototoxic drugs, noise trauma and acoustic neuroma","Cerumen impaction, otosclerosis, otitis media and tympanic membrane perforation","Cholesteatoma, exostoses, foreign body and ossicular necrosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All four damage the cochlea or the nerve. Note acoustic neuroma sits here because cranial nerve VIII is part of the sensorineural pathway even though the tumour is outside the cochlea. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which protein deficiency contributes to atopic dermatitis, and what does it reflect?","choices":["Insufficient elastin, reflecting loss of recoil","Insufficient keratohyalin, reflecting loss of the granular layer","Insufficient filaggrin, reflecting a compromised epidermal barrier","Insufficient collagen, reflecting fibroblast failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, alongside an overactive immune system and genetic, environmental and psychogenic factors. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which dermal cell mediates immunoglobulin E-mediated inflammation?","choices":["The fibroblast","The melanocyte","The keratinocyte","The mast cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and its degranulation is the mechanism behind both the wheal and urticaria. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Trauma versus a bee sting illustrates which distinction within acute inflammation?","choices":["Inflammation that resolves completely versus inflammation that becomes chronic","Serous versus fibrinous exudate","Endogenous versus exogenous toxic agents","Damage that is purely physical versus damage that involves activation of an immune response"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both produce acute inflammation, but only one recruits an immune response, which is the point of the pairing. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which mediator is distinguished from histamine and serotonin by also producing pain?","choices":["Immunoglobulin G","Fibrinogen","Bradykinin","Complement component C3b"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three raise permeability, but pain is listed only for bradykinin — along with a possible increase in leukocyte chemotaxis. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What defines a secondary skin lesion?","choices":["Any lesion that is palpable rather than flat","Any lesion that arises from malignant rather than from benign underlying disease processes","A modification or change of a primary lesion caused by infection, trauma or other factors","Any lesion appearing on a second body site after the first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, which is why a secondary lesion may or may not still resemble what it came from. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Is metastatic spread random?","choices":["No — but only because lymphatic drainage is anatomically fixed","No — it is determined by blood flow, receptors, and genetically determined fitness","Yes — circulating cells lodge in whichever capillary bed they reach first","Yes for haematogenous spread, no for lymphatic spread"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is the headline. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What do B lymphocytes do?","choices":["Cell-mediated killing of virus-infected cells through T cell receptors","Returning immune function to its normal operation after infection, and preventing autoimmunity","Releasing histamine to increase vascular permeability","Making antibodies against antigens, acting as antigen-presenting cells, and developing into memory B cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three are listed, and the B lymphocyte is described as playing a large role in the humoral immune response and being essential to adaptive immunity. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which two ototoxic effects are described as reversible or symptom-limited rather than permanent?","choices":["Noise trauma and presbycusis","Furosemide on the strial potential; salicylate on prestin","Gentamicin and tobramycin damage","Cisplatin and carboplatin damage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The loop diuretic effect on the strial potential is reversible, and the salicylate effect on the outer hair cell motor protein produces tinnitus. Neither destroys the cells outright. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which inherited defect underlies xeroderma pigmentosum, and what is the consequence?","choices":["Defective apoptosis genes, so damaged cells cannot self destruct; increased risk of lymphoma","Defective protooncogenes, so growth signalling is lost; increased risk of aplasia","Defective repair genes, so ultraviolet B damage cannot be repaired","Defective tumour suppressor genes, so cell growth cannot be inhibited; increased risk of nervous system tumours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the consequence is an increased risk of skin cancer in sun-exposed areas. It is a repair-gene disease rather than a tumour suppressor one. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"In the organ of Corti, what physically opens the tip-link channels on the hair cells?","choices":["Shearing of the stereocilia against the tectorial membrane","Stapes vibration transmitted directly to the hair cells","Direct pressure of endolymph on the hair cell body","Glutamate binding to the hair cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Basilar membrane displacement bends the stereocilia against the rigid tectorial membrane, and that mechanical deflection is what opens the channels. Everything upstream exists to produce that shear. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Eosinophils primarily deal with which category of infection, and predominate in which reactions?","choices":["Bacterial infection; predominating in suppurative inflammation","Parasitic infection; predominating in allergic reactions such as asthma, hay fever and hives","Viral infection; predominating in cell-mediated immunity","Fungal infection; predominating in chronic granulomatous inflammatory processes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Parasitic infection, with predominance in allergic reactions. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which three structures make up the uvea?","choices":["The choroid, the retina and the sclera","The ciliary body, the lens and the zonules","The iris, the cornea and the conjunctiva","The choroid, the ciliary body and the iris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the vascular middle tunic in full. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Eczematous dermatitis results from which type of inflammatory reaction?","choices":["Neutrophil-driven suppurative inflammation","Complement-mediated vessel destruction","T cell-mediated","Immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell degranulation alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a chronic condition with frequent or infrequent flares, known as the itch that rashes. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What do the prefixes leio- and rhabdo- mean in a sarcoma's name?","choices":["Leio- is bone; rhabdo- is cartilage","Leio- is glandular; rhabdo- is squamous","Leio- is smooth muscle; rhabdo- is skeletal muscle","Leio- is skeletal muscle; rhabdo- is smooth muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and adeno- means glandular, while the suffix -oma simply means tumour. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 14:43"},{"q":"What are the two pathophysiological causes of strabismus?","choices":["Lens opacification and loss of the red reflex","Extraocular muscle imbalance and cranial nerve palsy of the third, fourth or sixth nerves","Retinal detachment and vitreous haemorrhage","Raised intraocular pressure with progressive optic disc cupping and field loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those two mechanisms. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is amblyopia?","choices":["Ocular misalignment arising from extraocular muscle imbalance or nerve palsy","Progressive optic neuropathy from raised pressure","Opacification of the crystalline lens","Reduced best-corrected visual acuity from abnormal visual processing during the critical developmental period"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is a visual deficit rather than a mechanical one. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What drives the change from non-proliferative to proliferative diabetic retinopathy?","choices":["Rising intraocular pressure forces new vessels to form","Accumulation of lipofuscin and apolipoprotein beneath the pigment epithelium","Bacterial collagenase digestion of the retinal surface","Severe ischaemia upregulates vascular endothelial growth factor, producing neovascularisation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — ischaemia to growth factor to new vessels. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is cancer, and where does the word come from?","choices":["A neoplasm that has metastasized; from the Greek for wandering, because cells migrate","A precancerous lesion; from the Latin for disorder, because architecture is lost","A malignant neoplasm; from the Latin for crab","Any neoplasm at all; from the Latin for growth, because the mass enlarges without a stimulus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — so named because it adheres to any tissue it seizes upon and reaches out with claws, invading surrounding tissue. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where do impulses travel after leaving the optic nerve?","choices":["To the temporal cortex","To the frontal eye fields","To the superior colliculus alone","To the occipital cortex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — neural transmission runs to the occipital cortex. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What drives dizziness or lightheadedness, as distinct from vertigo?","choices":["Cerebral hypoperfusion or metabolic imbalance","Asymmetrical input between the two vestibular apparatus","Dislodged otoconia in the semicircular canals","Endolymphatic hydrops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. None of these is vestibular, which is why the sensation is of impending faint rather than of the room moving. Getting this distinction right is the first branch point in the whole workup. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Why are there three semicircular canals rather than one?","choices":["Each detects a different speed of rotation","Two detect rotation and one detects gravity","Redundancy in case one is damaged","They sit in three planes, so rotation in any direction is detected"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sagittal, coronal and transverse between them cover all rotational movement. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the fornix, and why does it matter mechanically?","choices":["The dense fibrous plate giving the eyelid its shape","The sphincteric muscle responsible for blinking","The circular junction where the conjunctiva meets the edge of the cornea","A flexible junctional cul-de-sac that permits free rotation of the globe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is its definition and its mechanical purpose. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is a papilloma?","choices":["A tumour with finger-like or warty projections from an epithelial surface","A tumour forming a glandular pattern or arising from a gland","A malignant tumour arising from supportive, mesenchymal tissue","A malignant epithelial tumour showing squamous cell differentiation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the projections are visible microscopically or macroscopically. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the cellular events of acute inflammation after stasis?","choices":["Transmigration, adhesion, aggregation, phagocytosis, chemotaxis","Aggregation, chemotaxis, transmigration, adhesion, phagocytosis","Leukocyte adhesion, transmigration, chemotaxis, aggregation, phagocytosis","Chemotaxis, phagocytosis, adhesion, transmigration, aggregation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adhesion to the endothelium precedes movement across it, then directed migration, gathering at the site, and finally ingestion. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are mesenchymal tumours derived from, and what tissues does that include?","choices":["Neural tissue: peripheral nerves, nerve sheaths and the central nervous system","Supportive tissue: connective tissue, adipose tissue, cartilage, smooth and striated muscle, and bone","Epithelial surfaces: skin, gastrointestinal lining, respiratory lining and glandular ducts","Haematopoietic tissue: bone marrow, lymph nodes, spleen and thymus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same origin gives sarcomas when the tumour is malignant. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which histological findings go with chronic inflammation?","choices":["White blood cells (lymphocytes and macrophages), proliferation of blood vessels, fibrosis and tissue necrosis","Neutrophil predominance with an absence of vascular proliferation","Eosinophil infiltration with mucus hypersecretion","Serous exudate with low protein content, no cellular infiltrate, and no proliferation of blood vessels"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All four are listed together as the histological associations of chronic inflammation. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the demographic and inheritance pattern of otosclerosis?","choices":["Young-to-middle-aged females, accelerated by pregnancy; 50% autosomal dominant","Commonest in young females but entirely sporadic","Commonest in older males, with no hereditary component","Commonest in children, autosomal recessive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pregnancy acceleration is the memorable part, and variable penetrance explains why the family history is often incomplete. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which nodal findings require immediate assessment to exclude lymphoma?","choices":["Persistent rubbery or matted nodes in older adults","Any palpable cervical node at all","Tender mobile nodes during a viral upper respiratory infection","Bilateral small nodes in a child with otitis media"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Reactive nodes are usually tender and follow a regional infection. Rubbery, matted, persistent and supraclavicular in an older patient is the combination that changes the level of concern. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which genes are named as promoting repair of damaged deoxyribonucleic acid?","choices":["BRCA-1 and BRCA-2","NF-1 and NF-2","Retinoblastoma and adenomatous polyposis coli","p16 and p53"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a minority of breast cancer patients carry an inherited mutation in one of them. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which glands are involved in an external as opposed to an internal hordeolum?","choices":["Both involve the tarsal plate itself rather than any gland","External involves the glands of Zeis or Moll; internal involves a meibomian gland","External involves a meibomian gland; internal involves the glands of Zeis or Moll","Both involve the lacrimal gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — gland by gland, that is the distinction. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In chronic inflammation, what roles do lymphocytes and plasma cells versus macrophages play?","choices":["Both populations act solely to proliferate blood vessels","Lymphocytes and plasma cells mediate antibody production; macrophages process and deliver antigen to immunocompetent cells","Lymphocytes release histamine; macrophages release serotonin","Lymphocytes phagocytose the cellular debris at the site, while macrophages produce and secrete the antibody"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibody production belongs to lymphocytes and plasma cells, antigen processing and delivery to macrophages. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A patient reacts to a nickel earring 48 to 72 hours after wearing it. Which mechanism does the delay reflect?","choices":["An immediate immunoglobulin E-mediated mast cell degranulation occurring upon the very first contact with the metal","Direct chemical injury requiring no prior exposure","Viral proliferation within basal keratinocytes","Delayed hypersensitivity requiring T cell migration after re-exposure to a hapten previously presented by Langerhans cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The delay is the time the cellular response takes, which is what distinguishes it from an immediate mast cell reaction. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is the suspected mechanism of koilonychia?","choices":["Overgrowth of connective tissue producing an opaque nail","Incomplete keratinization producing white spots","Impaired keratin synthesis associated with deficiency anaemia, weakening the nail plate into raised ridges that are thin and concave","A halt of keratin production producing horizontal transverse grooves across the surface of the nail plate itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the exact pathophysiology is unknown. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What does anaplasia mean?","choices":["Disordered growth of epithelium with retained architecture","Lack of differentiation","Close resemblance to the comparable normal cell","Failure of a primordial tissue to develop into a mature organ"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the extreme of the grading scale, with no resemblance to the normal cell. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What can astigmatism combine with, and what happens when it does?","choices":["Only presbyopia, and only after the age of forty","Only glaucoma, through a sustained rise in intraocular pressure over years","Nothing — it always occurs in isolation","Myopia or hyperopia, adding a focal-length error to the axis error"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Webster described the two problems stacking. — Lecture recording, 26 August 2026"},{"q":"Which cancers is Epstein Barr virus associated with, and which cells does it infect?","choices":["B cell lymphomas and nasopharyngeal carcinoma; it infects B lymphocytes","Hepatocellular carcinoma; it infects hepatocytes and causes chronic injury with regeneration","Cervical carcinoma; it infects cervical epithelium and integrates its genome","Gastric adenocarcinoma and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma; it colonizes the stomach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it immortalizes them, which is the key step, and it also infects epithelial cells of the oropharynx. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the primary aetiology of TRACTIONAL retinal detachment?","choices":["Choroidal melanoma","Proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Severe malignant hypertension","Posterior vitreous detachment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the neovascular membranes of proliferative disease are the classic traction source. — 4. Ophthalmic Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION_v2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What does an epidermal inclusion cyst arise from and contain?","choices":["A sebaceous gland, and it contains only squalene","A blood vessel, and it contains clotted blood","A hair follicle, and it contains keratin","A sweat gland, and it contains sodium and chloride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A cyst is an elevated, circumscribed, encapsulated lesion in the dermis or subcutaneous layer with liquid or semisolid contents. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How do vocal cord polyps differ from nodules in site and pathogenesis?","choices":["Unilateral fibrous calluses from chronic yelling","Bilateral fibrous calluses from chronic phonotrauma","Bilateral soft lesions at the anterior commissure from reflux","Unilateral, soft and pedunculated on the middle third, after one severe strain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. One event rather than chronic repetition, and the healing response is inflammatory rather than a callus. Site and laterality both differ from nodules. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why can an implanted medical device provoke an inflammatory response even when it is sterile?","choices":["Because implantation always causes bacterial contamination of surrounding tissue","Because sterilisation leaves residual endotoxin behind, and that endotoxin then acts as a chemotactic agent","Because the material becomes coated with immunoglobulin G and C3b, and immune cells recognise that coating and attach to it","Because the device physically obstructs lymphatic drainage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Opsonisation of the biomaterial is what makes it a target regardless of sterility. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which cells drive psoriasis, and which cytokines are involved?","choices":["Melanocytes proliferate at the dermal-epidermal junction","Active T cells infiltrating the epidermis stimulate keratinocyte proliferation and excessive cytokine production, particularly tumour necrosis factor alpha, interferon gamma and interleukin-12","Mast cells release histamine which drives keratinocyte proliferation","Langerhans cells present haptens to T cells, producing a delayed hypersensitivity reaction 48 to 72 hours after each exposure to the allergen in question every single time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is a chronic autoimmune inflammatory dermatosis, and skin cells multiply up to ten times faster than normal. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which cytokines drive nasal polyp formation, and which cell floods the tissue?","choices":["Interleukins 4, 5 and 13, with eosinophil influx","Interleukin 17, with mast cell influx","Interleukins 1, 6 and tumour necrosis factor, with neutrophil influx","Interferon gamma, with lymphocyte influx"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is type 2 inflammation, and naming the three interleukins is the molecular-mechanism objective in action. Polyps are benign, non-neoplastic oedematous masses from the sinus ostia or ethmoid air cells. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What enlarges the inferior turbinates, and what does rhinitis medicamentosa refer to?","choices":["Bacterial infection of the turbinate bone; osteomyelitis","Cartilage displacement off the midline; a congenital deformity","Venous engorgement, oedema or bony hypertrophy; decongestant rebound","Eosinophilic polyp formation; an allergic reaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three possible substrates, and the drug-induced one has its own name. It is the same rebound phenomenon that appears in ocular pharmacology &mdash; overuse of a vasoconstrictor leaving the tissue more congested than before. — 5. ENT Clinical Pathophysiology_STUDENT VERSION.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How do petechiae, purpura and ecchymoses differ?","choices":["By size — petechiae 1 to 2 millimetres or less, purpura 3 to 10 millimetres, ecchymoses greater than 10 millimetres — with similar underlying pathology","By mechanism — petechiae from capillary rupture, purpura from vessel dilatation, and ecchymoses from melanin deposition in the dermis rather than blood","By depth — petechiae epidermal, purpura dermal, ecchymoses subcutaneous","By blanchability — petechiae blanch, purpura and ecchymoses do not"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a single process of bleeding into the dermis described at three scales. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"How often does the epidermis turn over, and what modifies that rate?","choices":["Every 6 to 12 months, and considerably more rapid in older patients","Turnover ceases after adolescence","Every 30 to 60 days, and turnover is more rapid in younger patients","Every 24 to 48 hours, with no age dependence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Age-dependent turnover matters because it sets how quickly the epidermis can repair itself. — Pathophys Dermatology - Gopal 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Pseudomembranous inflammation consists of a superficial necrotic layer made up of which components?","choices":["Granulation tissue, proliferating fibroblasts and newly formed capillary networks throughout","Fibrin, inflammatory cells and debris, forming a membrane-like covering over the affected mucosa","Pus alone, without a fibrin component","Mucus, plasma and erythrocytes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That covering over the mucosa is what the term describes. — 1. Inflammation.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
